      "rev_n_11_16ad83_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_11_16ad83_1",
        "node_id": "n_11_16ad83",
        "thought_text": "Count the degree of each node and use combinatorial mathematics to calculate the number of optimal paths of length 2 by considering each node's neighbors. For each node, calculate the number of ways to choose 2 distinct neighbors, which represents the number of optimal paths of length 2 originating from that node. Sum these values across all nodes to get the total number of optimal paths of length 2.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8794579431639064776",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_11_16ad83_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_e8b39ab968",
        "created_at": 1794504693.8142602
      },
      "rev_n_5_f483c5_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_f483c5_1",
        "node_id": "n_5_f483c5",
        "thought_text": "Thought-2 (revised): Instead of performing a BFS from each node to explore up to two levels deep, we can use a more efficient approach by leveraging the properties of the tree structure. Since the graph is a tree (connected and acyclic), we can precompute the number of children for each node. Then, for each node, we can directly calculate the number of optimal paths of length 2 by considering the product of the number of children of its neighbors. This avoids redundant calculations and ensures correctness.",
